# Persistent Fixes — Do Not Silently Revert

## Core principle

When the user asks for a change or fix, treat it as **permanent** unless they
later explicitly ask to revert or replace it.

When touching a file that contains a prior user-requested fix:

- Preserve the fix. If you don't understand why something looks the way it
  does, ask before rewriting it.
- Do not "clean up" a line that looks different from its neighbours — it may be
  a deliberate correction of a prior bug.
- When doing large refactors that rewrite big sections of a file, re-check
  every line you produce against the current file on disk. Never replace a
  line with an earlier (pre-fix) version from memory.
- If you see a conspicuous inline comment warning about a known regression,
  never delete or reword it; it's load-bearing.

## Known regressions in this repo

### `background.js` — feature import paths

All feature files live in `./features/`. Every `importScripts` for a feature
**must** include the `features/` prefix. Missing the prefix causes Chrome to
fail service-worker registration with `Status code: 15`, which silently breaks
the entire extension.

```js
// BAD — regressed twice already
importScripts('unknown-ops-log.js');

// GOOD
importScripts('features/unknown-ops-log.js');
importScripts('features/remove-brain.js');
importScripts('features/customize-brain.js');
importScripts('features/add-action-ops.js');
importScripts('features/add-brain.js');
```

If you rewrite the top of `background.js`, keep the warning comment above the
block of feature `importScripts` calls.
